<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  KALFA adaptive-icon foreground: the "K" mark.

  Source is the web app's MASKABLE PWA icon, not the primary one:
  KALFA beta repo: public/icons/maskable-icon.svg. That file was already
  redrawn by the design source of truth specifically for masked contexts, and
  only its K path is taken here - the decorative
  <circle r=190 opacity=0.16> halo in that file is a soft-background effect
  for the PWA's own maskable rendering and is deliberately NOT reproduced;
  KALFA's flat #FF5A3C fill is a separate layer (ic_launcher_background.xml).
  The pathData below is copied VERBATIM from maskable-icon.svg and is
  expressed in that file's 512x512 coordinate space; the <group> maps it onto
  Android's 108x108dp canvas instead of re-typing the coordinates, so the
  shape stays provably identical to the source - diff the two strings to
  confirm.

  Why this glyph instead of the primary icon.svg one: the primary K is used
  verbatim (with a small centring nudge) for the legacy mipmap-*dpi/ rasters,
  where it is drawn full-bleed with no mask. The adaptive foreground is
  clipped by an OEM mask instead, and the guaranteed-safe region is not a
  square but a 66dp-DIAMETER CIRCLE centred on the 108dp canvas (the square in
  Android's own diagrams is a simplification - OEM masks are only guaranteed
  convex, and may reach as little as 33dp from centre along any radius,
  including the diagonals). The maskable glyph's proportions (265x216, aspect
  1.227) are flatter than the primary's (270x240, aspect 1.125), which suits a
  circular clip better; rendered size at the same 33dp guarantee comes out
  within ~1.5% of area either way, so this is a shape decision, not a size
  one.

  Centring is done on the glyph's BOUNDING-BOX centre, not its area-weighted
  centroid. The two disagree here: the centroid sits ~28 units left of the
  bbox centre because the solid stem outweighs the open diagonal arms. But
  centroid-centring was rendered and rejected - it leaves visible dead space
  on the right inside the safe circle and forces an ~10% smaller scale for no
  perceptual gain, because three of the K's four bounding-box corners are
  real ink vertices, not empty box corners, so the bbox centre already
  balances the actual silhouette.

  Geometry (verified against the maskable-icon.svg path):
    glyph vertices (512-space): (142,148) (214,148) (214,252) (318,148)
      (402,148) (288,260) (407,364) (313,364) (214,269) (214,364) (142,364)
    bounding box x[142,407] y[148,364] = 265 x 216, centred on (274.5,256).
    Farthest vertex from that centre is (142,148) and (407,364), each at
    distance sqrt(132.5^2+108^2) = 170.9393167dp (in source units) - real ink
    corners, confirmed by rendered proof (see contact sheet), not a
    worst-case empty-corner assumption.

    Target: no ink beyond 33.00dp radius from the canvas centre (54,54).
    scaleX/Y = floor7(33 / 170.9393167) = 0.1930509 (floored, not rounded, so
                                                       the realized radius is
                                                       guaranteed <= target)
    translateX = 54 - 274.5 * 0.1930509 =  1.007528
    translateY = 54 - 256   * 0.1930509 =  4.5789696

  A group's matrix applies scale before translate (pivot defaults to 0,0), so
  a point maps to s*p + t - identical to how the SVG source was proven via
  <g transform="translate(t) scale(s)">, which SVG composes right-to-left
  (scale first, then translate) - same s*p + t. That puts the mark's bbox at
  x[28.4208,79.5792] y[33.1505,74.8495] (51.16 x 41.70dp), centred on the
  canvas, and its single farthest ink point (source vertex 407,364, mapped to
  79.5792,74.8495) at radius 32.99998897dp from centre - inside the 33dp
  guarantee with a ~0.00001dp margin from floor-rounding, not from a deliberately
  smaller design (a rendered comparison at R=33/32/31/30dp showed 33 reads as
  confidently filled, not crowded - see contact sheet).

  ic_launcher.xml also references this drawable as the <monochrome> layer.
  Verified against Android's adaptive-icon docs: the monochrome layer uses
  the identical 108x108dp canvas and 66dp safe zone as the foreground, with
  no documented difference in weight or sizing, so reusing this drawable
  as-is is correct and a separate silhouette would be redundant.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">
    <group
        android:scaleX="0.1930509"
        android:scaleY="0.1930509"
        android:translateX="1.007528"
        android:translateY="4.5789696">
        <path
            android:fillColor="#FFFDF8"
            android:pathData="M142 148h72v104l104-104h84L288 260l119 104h-94l-99-95v95h-72V148Z" />
    </group>
</vector>
